// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BOARD GEOMETRY, read first-party from the vendor manual this module already cites.
//
// PROVENANCE, stated before the numbers because it is the fact that decides how they may be used.
// extdeps_external_authority_anchor names download.asrock.com/Manual/ALTRAD8UD-1L2T.pdf. That
// document was FETCHED AND READ by the authoring session — download.asrock.com answers 200 from
// this environment, unlike the general web, which is refused — so these are a first-party read of
// the manual and not the relayed grade extdeps.climbing_holds.atomik is stamped with. Section 1.4
// "Motherboard Layout" carries the outline as leader dimensions: 26.7 cm (10.5 in) by 24.4 cm
// (9.6 in).
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES. asrock_altrad8ud_1l2t_mechanical carries form_factor_label, a String reading
// "deep micro-ATX (9.6 x 10.5 in)". That row states a MEASUREMENT in prose, which DESIGN §4c names
// as the thing a typed carrier must hold instead — a String cannot be asked whether a chassis panel
// clears the board. The label is left standing because it also carries a FORM-FACTOR CLAIM, which
// is a different fact from an outline and is not established by these two lengths.
//
// THE AXES, AND AN ERROR THIS FILE SHIPPED ONCE. The board is 244 mm ALONG THE REAR I/O EDGE and
// 267 mm DEEP: the manual prints 24.4 cm (9.6 in) and 26.7 cm (10.5 in), and the vendor calls the
// form factor "deep micro-ATX (9.6 x 10.5 in)".
//
// An earlier revision transposed these and asserted the board was 267 mm WIDE against micro-ATX's
// 244 mm, concluding that a micro-ATX hole pattern would be laid out 23 mm too narrow. That was
// backwards and the conclusion inverts. Deep micro-ATX keeps the micro-ATX rear-I/O WIDTH exactly
// and extends the DEPTH — extdeps.standards.atx_2_2 deep_micro_atx_axis_note carries the relation —
// so the rear-edge hole columns are the standard micro-ATX ones and it is the FRONT row that moves
// outward by the extra 0.9 in. The error mattered: it is the difference between "the standard
// pattern does not apply" and "the standard pattern applies except at known positions".
//
// The outline is still NOT a conformance claim. Matching two edge dimensions is not the same as
// carrying the specification's hole grid, and extdeps.standards.atx_2_2 deliberately does not
// supply that grid either — the specification places the holes in a figure with no text layer.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
fn asrock_altrad8ud_board_frame() -> CoordinateFrameIdentity { coordinate_frame_identity(occurrence: 31) }

// Micrometres, for the reason std.spatial_frame records and extdeps.printing.fdm repeats: the
// quantum is chosen from the smallest distinction the frame must preserve, not from the largest
// value it must hold. Whole millimetres are sufficient for these two extents and are NOT sufficient
// for what will be drawn in this frame next — DIMM slot pitch and standoff positions — and a frame
// whose quantum is retro-fitted after coordinates exist in it silently rescales every one of them.
// x is ALONG THE REAR I/O EDGE (244 mm), y is DEPTH FROM THAT EDGE (267 mm). The axis assignment is
// stated here because the frame carries no axis names and the numbers alone do not disclose which is
// which — which is exactly how the transposition above survived review the first time.
fn asrock_altrad8ud_board_outline() -> OrthogonalConstruction<AxisAlignedRectangle2> {
  axis_aligned_rectangle2(
    min: point2(frame: asrock_altrad8ud_board_frame(), x: 0, y: 0),
    max: point2(frame: asrock_altrad8ud_board_frame(), x: 244000, y: 267000)
  )
}

data asrock_altrad8ud_board_outline_citation: FactCitation = FactCitation {
  fact: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "extdeps.boards.asrock_rack",
    decl_name: "asrock_altrad8ud_board_outline",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  },
  authority: extdeps_external_authority_anchor
}

// THE MOUNTING HOLES ARE NOT HERE, AND THEIR ABSENCE IS THE POINT.
//
// Section 1.4 draws the standoff holes as unlabelled circles on the layout figure and dimensions
// NONE of them: the manual states the outline and stops. So the coordinates a chassis actually
// needs are, for this subject, an OBLIGATION and not a fact — and this module refuses to hold them
// rather than deriving them from the micro-ATX pattern the form-factor label gestures at. That
// derivation is available, plausible, and would be wrong in a way nothing downstream could detect:
// it would place standoffs against a 244 mm-wide pattern on a 267 mm-wide board, produce a chassis
// that assembles until the last two screws, and cite this manual while doing it.
//
// DESIGN §5's absorbing fallback is precisely this move — substituting the superset, or the nearby
// standard, when the precise answer is unavailable — so the arm refuses and names what would clear
// it. The operator holds four of these boards and four more are inbound; caliper positions from a
// declared origin corner are a first-party measurement, admissible at the OperatorMeasured grade
// that extdeps.ampere.mt_collins_product_brief.platform already establishes for a tape-measured
// chassis depth. That is the trigger: measured standoff coordinates, not a second reading of this
// manual, which does not contain them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE STANDOFF POSITIONS: MODELLED AS ESTIMATES, NOT AS MEASUREMENTS.
//
// These are the seven mounting holes plus the one chassis standoff that must be REMOVED, read off
// an annotated overlay of the manual's section 1.4 layout figure. The figure is drawn to scale —
// its outline aspect ratio matches 10.5/9.6 to four figures — so it can be used as a ruler. The
// circles marking the holes are schematic markers rather than dimensioned features, so the reading
// carries roughly +/- 0.4 in.
//
// WHY THAT MAKES THEM ESTIMATES AND NOT MEASUREMENTS, which is the whole content of this block.
// +/- 0.4 in is +/- 10.16 mm. A standoff hole is about 4 mm across. The uncertainty is two and a
// half times the diameter of the thing being located, so these coordinates cannot place a hole,
// cannot drill a tray, and cannot be checked against by a part that must fit. Admitting them as
// measured values would put a number where no number is known, and every downstream part would
// inherit a precision nobody has.
//
// std.spatial_knowledge already draws exactly this distinction: an ESTIMATE is a region with no
// privileged point, carrying the method that produced it, and it is a different arm from a measured
// value with an uncertainty. So these are estimates, they carry the figure-reading method, and a
// consumer demanding MeasuredOnly standing — which placing a standoff does — refuses them. That
// refusal is the correct behaviour, not a gap.
//
// WHAT THEY ARE NEVERTHELESS GOOD FOR, and it is not nothing:
//   - they confirm the pattern is the ATX-family grid rather than something proprietary;
//   - they locate the REMOVE-THIS-STANDOFF position well enough to act on, because that decision
//     has a tolerance of "which standoff", not "where exactly";
//   - they size and place the SLOTTED standoff carriers of the FIT fixture, whose whole purpose is
//     to let the physical board establish the exact positions, which are then measured back and
//     frozen. A slot only needs to be in the right neighbourhood.
//
// COORDINATES: x is along the rear I/O edge, y is depth from that edge, matching the outline above,
// in micrometres. Converted from the source's inches at 25400 um/in. Both maxima fall inside the
// outline (229870 < 244000 and 237490 < 267000), which is a cheap consistency check the transposed
// axes would have failed.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

data asrock_altrad8ud_standoff_figure_authority: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: Uri {
    scheme: Https
    locator: "drive.google.com/file/d/1z36tmUWAwOwXWy6ASmaPWJNzKoZggBs8/view"
  }
}

type BoardStandoffEstimate sole_constructor {
  label: NonEmptyStr
  along_rear_edge: Micrometer
  depth_from_rear_edge: Micrometer
  disposition: StandoffDisposition
}

// A position where the board HAS a hole is a different fact from a position where a standard tray
// has a standoff the board does NOT use. Conflating them is how a live standoff ends up under a
// board with no hole above it, which is a short circuit rather than a fit problem.
type StandoffDisposition
  = BoardMountingHole
  | ChassisStandoffMustBeRemoved

fn board_standoff_estimate(
  label: NonEmptyStr,
  along_rear_edge: Micrometer,
  depth_from_rear_edge: Micrometer,
  disposition: StandoffDisposition,
) -> BoardStandoffEstimate {
  BoardStandoffEstimate {
    label: label,
    along_rear_edge: along_rear_edge,
    depth_from_rear_edge: depth_from_rear_edge,
    disposition: disposition,
  }
}

data asrock_altrad8ud_standoff_estimates: List<BoardStandoffEstimate> = [
  board_standoff_estimate(label: "H1", along_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 6350), depth_from_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 32766), disposition: BoardMountingHole),
  board_standoff_estimate(label: "H2", along_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 6350), depth_from_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 237490), disposition: BoardMountingHole),
  board_standoff_estimate(label: "H3", along_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 163830), depth_from_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 9906), disposition: BoardMountingHole),
  board_standoff_estimate(label: "H4", along_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 163830), depth_from_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 165100), disposition: BoardMountingHole),
  board_standoff_estimate(label: "H5", along_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 163830), depth_from_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 237490), disposition: BoardMountingHole),
  board_standoff_estimate(label: "H6", along_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 209550), depth_from_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 9906), disposition: BoardMountingHole),
  board_standoff_estimate(label: "H7", along_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 229870), depth_from_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 165100), disposition: BoardMountingHole),
  board_standoff_estimate(label: "X1", along_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 6350), depth_from_rear_edge: micrometer(count: 165100), disposition: ChassisStandoffMustBeRemoved),
]

data asrock_altrad8ud_standoff_estimate_region: Micrometer = micrometer(count: 10160)

data asrock_altrad8ud_standoff_estimate_method: NonEmptyStr = "Scale reading of the section 1.4 motherboard-layout figure via an annotated grid overlay (operator-supplied, cited above). The figure's outline is to scale — measured aspect ratio 1.0935 against 10.5/9.6 = 1.0938 — so it functions as a ruler at roughly 136 px/in; the hole markers are schematic circles, giving a half-width of about 0.4 in / 10160 um. ESTIMATE, not measurement: the region is 2.5x the diameter of a standoff hole, so these locate no hole and drill no tray."

data asrock_altrad8ud_standoff_measurement_obligation: NonEmptyStr = "STILL OPEN: standoff hole centres and diameter at a precision that can place a hole. The estimates above are a region, not a position, and the vendor manual dimensions none of the holes. Cleared by EITHER a reading of ATX specification Figure 3 by an instrument that can extract vector artwork — extdeps.standards.atx_2_2 records that this session could not, since the figure has no text layer — OR caliper positions from a declared origin corner on a held board. Until then the FIT fixture carries SLOTTED standoff carriers and the board itself establishes the positions, which are then measured back and frozen."
